Persistent layout work admits a private local configuration and separates
one title object store from target-specific executable packages. A read-only
local audit checked 329 manifest digest names and 1,130 canonical payload
entries without mismatches; 148,368,792 redundant logical inode bytes are
candidates for deduplication, not measured disk savings. The evidence lives
in the ignored persistent reference ledger. The engine, assets and importer
suites passed 220 tests. Shared-object publication and configured packaging
still require the full validation gate and a verified legacy migration.
No existing payload or original was moved or deleted.

Fresh validation 1795647945634452273-176012 passed 1,413 test
executions and all six portable core targets. Synthetic shared-store
regressions verify one unchanged physical payload inode across distinct
manifests, fail-closed changed objects and rejection of legacy directories
inserted before or during staging. Legacy readers remain supported. The
actual owner asset store and launch catalogue have not been migrated;
configured package execution on the owner's data remains open.

Configured package publication now rejects overlapping resolved asset and
binary paths before building. Two tiny fixture tests read a real private
.env, exercise a common deployment root, publish a package with separate
asset bytes, reject repeat publication and verify the asset bytes remain
unchanged. Unsupported target, absent bin parent and both containment
orders fail before publication. These are lexical checks; trusted local
ancestors remain required.

On 2026-11-25 the owner-configured GNU/Linux x86-64 output was created
under its existing trusted parent. The 3,529,168-byte executable and
108 supporting files totalled 4,043,088 bytes; the independent SHA-256
matched the receipt. File modes were 0700 for the executable and 0600
